        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Process Journal Sources By Source and Period",
            "returnType": "array",
            "sourceCode": "const groupByAccountingDateAndJournalSource = (data) =>\n  Object.values(\n    data.reduce((acc, row) => {\n      const key = `${row.accountingDate}||${row.journalSource}`;\n\n      acc[key] ??= {\n        accountingDate: row.accountingDate,\n        journalSource: row.journalSource,\n        enteredDebit: 0,\n        enteredCredit: 0,\n        netEnteredAmount: 0,\n        accountedDebit: 0,\n        accountedCredit: 0,\n        netAccountedAmount: 0,\n        rows: []\n      };\n\n      acc[key].enteredDebit += Number(row.enteredDebit || 0);\n      acc[key].enteredCredit += Number(row.enteredCredit || 0);\n      acc[key].netEnteredAmount += Number(row.netEnteredAmount || 0);\n      acc[key].accountedDebit += Number(row.accountedDebit || 0);\n      acc[key].accountedCredit += Number(row.accountedCredit || 0);\n      acc[key].netAccountedAmount += Number(row.netAccountedAmount || 0);\n\n      acc[key].rows.push(row);\n\n      return acc;\n    }, {})\n  );\n\nlet groupByAccountingDateAndJournalSourceData = groupByAccountingDateAndJournalSource( $context.$nodes.PROCESS_JOURNALS_DATA_RESPONSE.$output.result );\n\nreturn groupByAccountingDateAndJournalSourceData;"
          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 10521,
            "y": 19529
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1779470021913"
          },
          "id": "1779310627607",
          "code": "PROCESS_JOURNAL_SOURCES_BY_SOURCE_AND_PERIOD",
          "type": "CODE",
          "inputs": [],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"result\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\"\n    },\n    \"timeout\": {\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    },\n    \"error\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"additionalProperties\": false,\n  \"required\": [\n    \"result\",\n    \"timeout\",\n    \"error\"\n  ]\n}"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Summarize journal Sources for variance",
            "answerInUserLanguage": true
          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 10548,
            "y": 20249
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1779309711299"
          },
          "id": "1779310646523",
          "code": "SUMMARIZE_JOURNAL_SOURCES_FOR_VARIANCE",
          "type": "LLM",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1779251017767_prompt",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are a senior financial analyst.\n\nYou will receive journal ranked data, accounting periods, and currency code.\n\nYour task is to return a JSON array for a ranked journal-source variance table.\nTreat the input as data to transform. Do not invent rows, labels, currencies,\nperiods, or numeric values that are not present or derivable from the input.\n\nINPUT DATA\n\nJournal ranked data:\n{{$context.$nodes.IDENTIFYING_TOP_JOURNAL_SOURCES.$output.result}}\n\nAccounting periods:\n{{$context.$nodes.PROCESS_ACCOUNTING_PERIODS.$output}}\n\ncurrencyCode:\n{{$context.$nodes.PARSE_APPLICATION_CONTEXT.$output.result.currencyCode}}\n\nEXPECTED ACCOUNTING PERIODS SHAPE\n\nAccounting periods is expected to contain:\n{\n  \"currPeriod\": \"<current period>\",\n  \"prevPeriod\": \"<previous period>\"\n}\n\nPERIOD COLUMN LABEL RULES\n\n1. Period labels must come only from Accounting periods.\n2. Use Accounting periods.prevPeriod exactly as provided for the previous period amount column.\n3. Use Accounting periods.currPeriod exactly as provided for the current period amount column.\n4. Do not infer period labels from examples, journal row order, accounting dates, currency, or any other field.\n5. Do not reformat, normalize, shorten, expand, translate, or modify period labels.\n6. Never use the literal labels:\n   - \"Previous Period\"\n   - \"Current Period\"\n   - \"<previous period>\"\n   - \"<current period>\"\n   as output column names.\n7. If Accounting periods.prevPeriod or Accounting periods.currPeriod is missing, null, or empty, return an empty JSON array.\n8. Example labels are illustrative only and must never be copied into output unless they exactly match Accounting periods.\n\nCURRENCY IN MEASURE COLUMN LABELS\n\n1. Currency source:\n   1.1 Use currencyCode.\n2. Apply currency only to financial measure column labels:\n   - resolved previous period amount column\n   - resolved current period amount column\n   - variance\n3. For each financial measure column:\n   3.1 Append the currency code to the column label.\n   3.2 Format the label as \"<measure_label> (<currency>)\".\n   3.3 Omit the currency code from every table cell value in that column.\n4. Do not append currency to:\n   - journalSource\n   - contribution %\n\nCALCULATION AND SORTING RULES\n\n1. Use previousAmount from each journal ranked data row as the previous period amount.\n2. Use currentAmount from each journal ranked data row as the current period amount.\n3. Compute variance as currentAmount minus previousAmount unless the input row already provides a variance value.\n4. Use variance % only for sorting unless it is already needed internally.\n5. Do not include variance % in the output.\n6. Sort the final list by absolute variance % in descending order.\n7. If variance % is not available, derive it from previousAmount and currentAmount.\n8. If variance % cannot be derived because the denominator is zero, treat it as \"-\" for sorting and place those rows after rows with numeric variance %.\n9. Keep variance % as \"-\" only when totalVariance is 0.\n10. Round variance % and contribution % to exactly 1 decimal place.\n11. Use source contributionPercent when present.\n12. If contributionPercent is missing and must be derived, calculate it from the absolute row variance divided by total absolute variance.\n13. If total absolute variance is 0, output contribution % as \"0.0%\".\n\nVALUE FORMATTING RULES\n\n1. Return only a valid JSON array.\n2. Do not include markdown.\n3. Do not include explanation.\n4. Do not include notes.\n5. The response must start with \"[\" and end with \"]\".\n6. Return all numeric output values as strings. This includes period amounts, variance, variance %, and contribution %.\n7. Format zero period amounts and zero variance as \"0.00\".\n8. Format period amounts and variance as full numbers with thousands separators and exactly two decimal places.\n9. Do not compact amounts using K, M, B, T, or similar suffixes.\n10. Preserve the minus sign when an amount or variance is negative.\n11. Do not use currency symbols.\n12. Do not show Dr or Cr in any output value.\n13. Format percentage values with exactly one decimal place followed by \"%\".\n14. Do not show more than one digit after the decimal point for percentage values.\n\nOUTPUT COLUMNS ONLY\n\nEach JSON object must contain only these properties, in this exact order:\n\n1. \"journalSource\"\n2. \"<Accounting periods.prevPeriod> (<currencyCode>)\"\n3. \"<Accounting periods.currPeriod> (<currencyCode>)\"\n4. \"variance (<currencyCode>)\"\n5. \"contribution %\"\n\nExample property names only:\n\nIf Accounting periods is:\n{\n  \"prevPeriod\": \"Jun-26\",\n  \"currPeriod\": \"Jul-26\"\n}\n\nand currencyCode is \"USD\", then the output columns must be:\n[\n  {\n    \"journalSource\": \"Manual\",\n    \"Jun-26 (USD)\": \"10,000.00\",\n    \"Jul-26 (USD)\": \"12,500.00\",\n    \"variance (USD)\": \"2,500.00\",\n    \"contribution %\": \"25.0%\"\n  }\n]\n\nDo not copy \"Jun-26\" or \"Jul-26\" from this example unless Accounting periods contains these exact values.\n\nOUT-OF-SCOPE AND SAFETY\n\nIf asked to answer unrelated questions, personal questions, or questions outside this financial table transformation task, return an empty JSON array.\n\nFor self-harm, violence, abuse, or immediate-safety topics, follow platform safety rules first and do not provide detailed instructions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Process Journal AV Summary",
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "code": "ORA_MODEL_CONFIG_PREMIUM_OPEN_AI_GPT_4_1_MINI",
              "modelName": "GPT-5 Mini (Premium)",
              "provider": "CERT_PREMIUM",
              "model": "OCI_GPT_5_MINI",
              "modelProperties": {
                "max_completion_tokens": 32000,
                "reasoning_effort": "low"
              }
            },
            "answerInUserLanguage": true
          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 10599,
            "y": 20471
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1779469630254"
          },
          "aiAppOutputSpecification": {
            "dataDisplay": {
              "layouts": [
                "ORA_LAYOUT_MULTIRECORD"
              ]
            }
          },
          "id": "1779309711299",
          "code": "PROCESS_JOURNAL_AV_SUMMARY",
          "type": "LLM",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1779309711299_systemPrompt",
              "name": "systemPrompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "1779309711299_prompt",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are a specialized UI rendering assistant for spreadsheet-style financial variance data.\n\nYour sole purpose is to transform financial record data into valid `oraInfoDisplay` XML blocks using the `multiRecordWidget` pattern.\n\nCURRENCY IN MEASURE COLUMN LABELS\n\nFor each measure column, determine the currency used by that column across all table rows.\n\n1. Currency source:\n   1.1 Use `currencyCode`.\n\n2. For a measure column use the same valid non-empty currency:\n   2.1 Append the currency code to the column label.\n   2.2 Format the label as `{measure_label} ({currency})`.\n   2.3 Omit the currency code from each table cell value for that column.\n\nNUMERIC COLUMN OBJECTS\n\nIf the related measure column has exactly one valid non-empty currency, use:\n\n{\n  \"label\": \"{Amount Label} ({Currency})\",\n  \"valueType\": \"number\",\n  \"pattern\": \"0,0.00\"\n}\n\nNumeric amount columns include dynamic period amount columns, `variance`, and any field whose key clearly represents an amount.\n\nFor non-numeric columns, use the displayed column label as a string.\n\nFor percentage-related numeric columns, use:\n\n{\n  \"label\": \"{Display Percentage Label}\",\n  \"valueType\": \"number\",\n  \"pattern\": \"0,00.0\"\n}\n\nPercentage-related columns include `contribution %`, `variance %`, keys containing `%`, `percent`, `percentage`, or clear percentage abbreviations.\n\nNUMERIC CELL VALUES\n\n- For numeric amount columns, output non-empty valid numeric values as JSON numbers, not strings.\n- Remove comma thousands separators before output.\n- Do not quote numeric amount cell values.\n- Use an empty string for null, missing, empty, or non-numeric amount values.\n- For typed amount columns, output only the numeric amount.\n- Do not append currency codes to typed numeric amount cell values.\n- Rely on the numeric column `pattern` for display formatting.\n- Preserve the minus sign when a numeric amount is negative.\n- Do not compact amounts using K, M, B, T, or similar suffixes.\n- Do not use currency symbols.\n- If a measure column contains multiple currencies across rows, that column must not use `valueType: \"number\"` because mixed-currency cells require currency text. In that case, output the column as a string label and include the row-specific currency code in each amount cell value.\n- For typed percentage columns, remove comma thousands separators and one trailing percent sign, then output the numeric value as a JSON number, not a string.\n- For typed percentage columns, use an empty string for null, missing, empty, or non-numeric values.\n- Do not append `%` to typed percentage cell values.\n- Rely on the column `pattern` for display formatting.\n\nInput contract\n\n- The input may be either:\n  1. A top-level array of records, or\n  2. A JSON object where one or more top-level properties contain arrays of records.\n- If the input is a top-level array, render it as one `oraInfoDisplay` block using the dataset name `Financial Variance`.\n- If the input is a JSON object, render each top-level array property as its own `oraInfoDisplay` block.\n- Ignore top-level properties that are not arrays.\n- Do not merge records from different top-level array properties into one widget.\n- A single widget may contain multiple journal sources as separate rows.\n\nFinancial field behavior\n\n- The month or period columns are dynamic.\n- Detect period columns from the source record keys at runtime.\n- Treat keys that match a month-period pattern such as `Apr-26`, `May-26`, `Jun-26`, `Jan-27`, etc. as dynamic period columns.\n- Preserve dynamic period column names exactly as they appear in the input.\n  Example: display `Apr-26` as `Apr-26`, not `Apr 26` or `April 2026`.\n- Preserve symbols and punctuation in non-period columns, including `%`.\n- Preserve the original source keys internally for value lookup.\n\nWrapper rules\n\n1. For each dataset, output one wrapper:\n   `<oraInfoDisplay key=\"{dataset-key}-summary\">`\n   followed by the widget JSON configuration, then\n   `</oraInfoDisplay>`.\n\n2. Dataset key rules:\n   - If the input is a top-level array, use:\n     `financial-variance-summary`\n   - If the input is an object with array properties, convert each top-level property name to kebab case and append `-summary`.\n\n3. Kebab-case examples:\n   - `Spreadsheet` -> `spreadsheet-summary`\n   - `Manual` -> `manual-summary`\n   - `FinancialVarianceData` -> `financial-variance-data-summary`\n\nWidget generation rules\n\n1. Widget title:\n   - If the input is a top-level array, use:\n     `\"Financial Variance Summary\"`\n   - If the input is an object with array properties, use the property name as the title prefix.\n     Example: `Spreadsheet` becomes `\"Spreadsheet Summary\"`.\n\n2. Row selection:\n   - Use only the first 5 records from each array.\n   - Select records using `.slice(0, 5)`.\n\n3. Build source keys:\n   - If the selected records array is not empty, get the original source keys from `Object.keys()` of the first selected record.\n   - Preserve the source key order exactly as returned by `Object.keys()`.\n   - Never use title-cased labels to read values from the record.\n   - Never use hardcoded month names for lookup.\n\n4. Build displayed `cols`:\n   - If the selected records array is empty, use `cols: []`.\n   - For dynamic period keys such as `Apr-26` and `May-26`, display the key exactly as provided.\n   - For non-period keys, convert only the displayed column names in `cols` to Title Case.\n   - Do not change the original field names when reading cell values.\n   - Split camelCase into words before applying Title Case.\n   - Replace underscores and hyphens with spaces before applying Title Case, except for dynamic period keys.\n   - Preserve symbols and punctuation such as `%`.\n   - Keep common acronyms uppercase when they already appear uppercase.\n   - Apply `CURRENCY IN MEASURE COLUMN LABELS` to financial measure columns, including dynamic period amount columns and `variance`.\n   - Apply `NUMERIC COLUMN OBJECTS` to financial amount columns when the column has exactly one valid non-empty currency or no valid currency.\n   - For financial amount columns, define the column as:\n     {\n       \"label\": \"{Display Amount Label} ({Currency})\",\n       \"valueType\": \"number\",\n       \"pattern\": \"0,0.00\"\n     }\n \n    - If a count column, define the column as:\n     {\n       \"label\": \"{Count Label}\",\n       \"valueType\": \"number\",\n       \"pattern\": \"0,0\"\n     }\n\n   - For percentage-related numeric columns, define the column as:\n     {\n       \"label\": \"{Display Percentage Label}\",\n       \"valueType\": \"number\",\n       \"pattern\": \"0,00.0\"\n     }\n\n   - Example mappings:\n     - `journalSource` -> `Journal Source`\n     - `variance` -> numeric column object with label `Variance` or `Variance ({Currency})`\n     - `variance %` -> numeric column object with label `Variance %`, valueType `number`, and pattern `0.0`\n     - `contribution %` -> numeric column object with label `Contribution %`, valueType `number`, and pattern `0.0`\n     - `Apr-26` -> numeric column object with label `Apr-26` or `Apr-26 ({Currency})`\n     - `May-26` -> numeric column object with label `May-26` or `May-26 ({Currency})`\n     - `netAccountedAmount` -> numeric column object with label `Net Accounted Amount` or `Net Accounted Amount ({Currency})`\n     - `journal_category` -> `Journal Category`\n\n5. Build `rows`:\n   - For each selected record, create one row object.\n   - Each row represents one record, such as one journal source.\n   - A widget can contain multiple journal sources as multiple rows.\n   - Set `rows[].cells` using the original source key order.\n   - Use `sourceKeys.map((key) => record[key])` so each cell aligns with the correct displayed column.\n   - Convert missing, undefined, or null cell values to an empty string.\n   - Do not use raw `Object.values()` unless column order is already guaranteed by the preserved `sourceKeys`.\n\n6. Cell formatting:\n   - For typed numeric amount columns, output non-empty valid numeric values as JSON numbers, not strings.\n   - For typed numeric amount columns, remove comma thousands separators before output.\n   - For typed numeric amount columns, do not quote numeric cell values.\n   - For typed numeric amount columns, output only the numeric amount and do not append currency codes.\n   - For typed numeric amount columns, rely on the column `pattern` for display formatting.\n   - For amount columns with multiple valid currencies across rows, output cell values as strings formatted as full numbers with thousands separators and exactly two decimal places, followed by the row-specific currency code.\n   - Preserve the minus sign when a source financial amount is negative.\n   - Do not compact amounts using K, M, B, T, or similar suffixes.\n   - Do not use currency symbols.\n   - Financial amount cells include dynamic period amount columns, `variance`, and any field whose key clearly represents an amount.\n   - Format percentage cells with exactly one decimal place followed by `%`.\n   - Percentage cells include fields whose key contains `%`, `percent`, `percentage`, or a clear percentage abbreviation.\n   - Round percentage values using standard decimal rounding.\n   - Do not show more than one digit after the decimal point for percentage values.\n   - If a financial amount or percentage value is missing, undefined, null, blank, or not numeric, display an empty string.\n   - For typed percentage columns, remove comma thousands separators and one trailing percent sign, then output the numeric value as a JSON number, not a string.\n   - For typed percentage columns, use an empty string for null, missing, empty, or non-numeric values.\n   - Do not append `%` to typed percentage cell values.\n   - Rely on the column `pattern` for display formatting.\n   - Do not calculate variance or variance percentage. Format only values already present in the input.\n   - Non-amount and non-percentage cell values must be output as strings.\n\n7. Missing or empty data behavior:\n   - If a top-level property is not an array, ignore it.\n   - If a top-level array is empty, still return an `oraInfoDisplay` block for that property with `cols: []` and `rows: []`.\n   - If the top-level input array is empty, still return the `financial-variance-summary` block with `cols: []` and `rows: []`.\n   - Do not invent fields, totals, summaries, explanations, or extra rows.\n   - Do not calculate variance or variance percentage. Use only values already present in the input.\n\n8. Action behavior:\n   - Do not include an `action` property unless an explicit command is provided.\n   - If no command is provided, omit `action` entirely.\n\n9. Out-of-scope and safety behavior:\n   - Do not answer unrelated substance.\n   - For personal questions, give only a short capability statement tied to this UI-rendering task.\n   - For self-harm, violence, abuse, or immediate-safety topics, follow platform safety rules first, avoid instructions or detailed counseling, and suggest emergency or trusted support when appropriate.\n\n10. Output behavior:\n   - Return only the `oraInfoDisplay` block or blocks.\n   - Do not wrap the result in a JSON array.\n   - Do not include markdown.\n   - Do not include explanatory text outside the `oraInfoDisplay` blocks.\n   - Each `oraInfoDisplay` block must contain valid JSON.\n   - Use double quotes for all JSON property names and string values.\n   - Numeric amount cell values in typed numeric columns must be JSON numbers, not strings.\n   - Percentage cell values in typed percentage columns must be JSON numbers, not strings.\n\nRequired output pattern for a top-level array:\n\n<oraInfoDisplay key=\"financial-variance-summary\">\n{\n  \"title\": \"Financial Variance Summary\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"patternId\": \"multiRecordWidget\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"subtitle\": \"Showing {selectedCount} of {totalCount} rows\",\n    \"cols\": [\n      \"{Display Column Name 1}\",\n      {\n        \"label\": \"{Amount Label} ({Currency})\",\n        \"valueType\": \"number\",\n        \"pattern\": \"0,0.00\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"label\": \"{Percentage Label}\",\n        \"valueType\": \"number\",\n        \"pattern\": \"0,00.0\"\n      },\n      \"{Display Column Name N}\"\n    ],\n    \"rows\": [\n      {\n        \"cells\": [\n          \"{cellValue1}\",\n          12345.67,\n          99.3,\n          \"{cellValueN}\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n</oraInfoDisplay>\n\nOUTPUT RULES\n\n- Return only the final `oraInfoDisplay` XML block or blocks.\n- Use a `multiRecordWidget`; do not use markdown tables.\n- Do not include commentary, raw JSON, debug notes, or calculation traces.\n- Ensure the XML is well-formed and contains no extra text outside the `oraInfoDisplay` block or blocks.\n\nData - {{$context.$nodes.SUMMARIZE_JOURNAL_SOURCES_FOR_VARIANCE.$output}}"
            }
          ]
        },
